          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Orbit method
            (or
            = succeed "," do
              %em Kirillov’s method
            = succeed ")" do
              %em method of coadjoint orbits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+representation+theory"} geometric representation theory
            concerned with identifying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+representations"} unitary representations
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
            with the canonical
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actions"} actions
            on spaces of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            of certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundles"} line bundles
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coadjoint+orbits"} coadjoint orbits
            of the Lie group. In terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            this realizes
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representations"} representations
            as actions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+gauge+groups"} global gauge groups
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+operators"} quantum operators
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+quantum+states"} spaces of quantum states
            under
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
          %p
            More in detail, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+dual+space"} linear dual space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^*
            of a (finite-dimensional real)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            has a canonical structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            – its
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie-Poisson+structure"} Lie-Poisson structure
            –, namely for any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in \mathfrak{g}^*
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ⟨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\{ f, g\}(a)
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  \\big\langle
                  [\mathrm{d} f_a, \mathrm{d} g_a]
                  ,\,
                  a
                  \\big\rangle
                  \\,.
          %p
            This Poisson manifold is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foliation"} foliated
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+leaves"} symplectic leaves
            which are the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coadjoint+orbits"} coadjoint orbits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundles"} line bundles
            in question are the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundles"} prequantum line bundles
            of these
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic manifolds
          %p
            Hence, in the language of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            with the
            = succeed "-action" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+quantum+states"} spaces of quantum states
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanical+system"} classical mechanical system
            with a global
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry"} symmetry
          %p Many important classes of unitary representations are obtained by that method.
          %p
            Notably in the case of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Lie+groups"} compact Lie groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flag+manifolds"} flag manifolds
            and the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel-Weil+theorem"} Borel-Weil theorem
            says that under certain further conditions the expected unitary representations are obtained.
          %p
            The case of non-compact Lie groups is much less well understood, see for instance
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#GrahamVogan"} Graham &amp; Vogan 1999
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Vogan99"} Vogan 1999
          %h2#DefinitionsAndConstructions Definitions and constructions
          %p
            We list and discuss the basic notions, definitions and constructions in the context of the orbit method. A useful review is also in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Beasley11"} Beasley 2011, section 4
          %h3#the_group_and_its_lie_algebra The group and its Lie algebra
          %p
            Throughout, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
